{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Everything in our universe is in motion. Kinematics involves describing motion through properties such as position, time, velocity, and acceleration.",
      "focus": "describing motion through properties such as position"
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "If an object moves relative to a frame of reference, its position changes. This change in position is called displacement.",
      "focus": "This change in position is called displacement"
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Displacement is a vector quantity. It is written as final position minus initial position, and its standard international units are meters.",
      "focus": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And again, as before, this component can be positive, zero, or negative, depending on the sign of delta x."
    },
    {
      "source": "S5",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Average speed is not necessarily the same as the magnitude of the average velocity. If a trip ends where it started, displacement and average velocity are zero.",
      "focus": "average speed is not necessarily the same as"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The instantaneous velocity of an object is the limit of the average velocity as the elapsed time approaches zero.",
      "focus": "limit of the average velocity as the elapsed time approaches zero, or the derivative of x with respect to t: v(t) = d/dt x(t). (3.4) Like average velocity, instantaneous velocity is a vector with dimension of length per time. The instantaneous velocity at a specific time point t_0 is the rate of change of the position function, which is the slope of the position function x(t) at t_0. Figure 3.6 shows how the average velocity v_bar = delta x over delta t between two times approaches the instantaneous velocity at t_0. The instantaneous velocity is shown at time t_0, which happens to be at the maximum of the position function. The slope of the position graph is zero at this point, and thus the instantaneous velocity is zero. At other times, t_1, t_2, and so on, the instantaneous velocity is not zero because the slope of the position graph would be positive or negative. If the position function had a minimum, the slope of the position graph would also be zero, giving an instantaneous velocity of zero there as well. Thus, the zeros of the velocity function give the minimum and maximum of the position function."
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "When calculating instantaneous velocity, we need to specify the explicit form of the position function.",
      "focus": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S6",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "In a graph of position versus time, the instantaneous velocity is the slope of the tangent line at a given point.",
      "focus": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And that's how we describe instantaneous velocity at some specific time. If we were now being a little bit more general, we could just say that v at any time t is the limit delta t goes to 0, delta x over delta t, all direction i hat."
    },
    {
      "source": "S3",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Instantaneous speed is found by taking the absolute value of instantaneous velocity, and it is always positive.",
      "focus": "Instantaneous speed is found by taking the absolute value of instantaneous velocity, and it is always positive."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S3",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes. It has both magnitude and direction.",
      "focus": "Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes"
    },
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Instantaneous acceleration is the acceleration at a specific instant in time. We calculate the average acceleration between two points and let the time interval approach zero.",
      "focus": "acceleration at a specific instant in time"
    },
    {
      "source": "S5",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "In a graph of velocity versus time, instantaneous acceleration is the slope of the tangent line.",
      "focus": "instantaneous acceleration is the slope of the tangent line"
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And this quantity here, which is delta v over delta t, again, we can even call, as we said before, we can call this average acceleration. But what we're interested in is as we take the limit as delta t goes to 0, then the slope changes, you can see. And we continue this limiting process until we shrink delta t down to 0 and that what we have here is this is the slope of the tangent line."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "If we know the functional form of velocity, we can calculate instantaneous acceleration at any time point in the motion.",
      "focus": "If we know the functional form of velocity"
    }
  ]
}

